## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical zero-trust for admin access, mirroring real enterprise risk of credential theft and lateral movement in hybrid environments.

## Key Concepts
- Zero-trust, device posture, MFA, PIM, Just-In-Time
- Jump hosts, session recording, RBAC, break-glass, SIEM/audit

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-policy: grant only if posture true and MFA passed
if (device.posture && user.mfa && time.inWindow) {
  grantAccess();
} else {
  denyAccess();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure effectiveness (MTTR, number of break-glass events)?
- How would this adapt to cloud-native IAM vs PAM solutions across AWS/Azure?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-cloud IAM, data-centric controls, and JIT elevation at beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Identity federation and ROLES vs ATTRIBUTES
- Data-labeling and masking for sensitive data
- Ephemeral credentials and cross‑cloud logging
- Onboarding/offboarding automation and pre-deploy checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example policy skeleton
const policy = {
  Version: '2012-10-17',
  Statement: [
    {Effect: 'Allow', Action: ['s3:GetObject'], Resource: 'arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*', Condition: {'StringEquals': {'datasetLabel': 'PII'}}}
  ]
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect privilege creep across clouds?
- Which logs and alerts would you centralize and why?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests threat modeling and practical controls for log integrity across cloud boundaries, a common fintech risk.

## Key Concepts
- Log integrity, STRIDE/PASTA, SBOM, log signing, KMS, append-only storage, WORM, cross-cloud replication, SIEM/SOAR, tabletop exercises

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo code for signing log entries
function signLogEntry(entry, key) {
  const signature = crypto.sign("SHA256", Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(entry)), key);
  return { entry, signature };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify integrity of logs during audits across clouds?
- How would you coordinate key rotation and revocation in a distributed SIEM?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CISSP alignment on secure messaging design: threat modeling, cryptography, device attestation, revocation, and incident response, across multiple platforms.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end encryption and forward secrecy
- Device attestation and binding keys to a trusted device
- Key rotation, revocation, and audit logging
- Threat modeling methods (STRIDE/PASTA)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: rotate per-user keys (simplified)
async function rotateUserKey(userId) {
  const newKey = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({name:'AES-GCM', length:256}, true, ['encrypt','decrypt']);
  await storeKey(userId, newKey);
  await revokeOldKeys(userId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate key compromise containment in a live system?
- What logs and metrics would you collect to support forensics without exposing plaintext data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crypto agility, cross-cloud governance, and practical CISSP controls for data at rest and in transit.

## Key Concepts
- Crypto agility and KMS key hierarchies across clouds
- Envelope encryption, per-tenant DEKs, field-level encryption
- Key rotation, revocation, auditability, incident response
- Data residency and edge deployment considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rotation workflow
rotateMasterKey(cloud, keyId) {
  wrapNewDEKWithMasterKey(keyId);
  rewrapAllDataKeys();
  validateNoDowntime();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test key rotation impact on latency and availability?
- What would you monitor to detect key-compromise attempts and how would you respond?
